# erasync

erasync is a file syncing tool that uses dm-era to determine what data needs
to be replicated.

Traditional tools, like rsync, computes hashes across the entire file, both
locally and on the destination, in order to determine what changed and what
needs to be transfered.
While that works well for most use cases it does not work very well for
very large files with small change-sets, such as large databases and VM images
and such.

"I spent boundless CPU and I/O bandwidth to crunch hashes on my TB sized files
 just to figure out I just need to copy 4kb across.
  --ronnie"

In order to do so you need to have dm-era configured and in use on both SRC
and DST and then erasync will use the dm-era change-log to determine what
parts of the files are modified and needs to be synced.

First copy is always the entire file but later copies are just the blocks
that have changed.
Blocks written on the destination since the last copy are copied back
too: in files whose mtime moved, or in every file with --check-dest.

erasync copies a directory SRC into a directory DEST, or a single file.
A file is written to DEST, or into DEST when DEST is a directory.
It replicates regular files, directories, symlinks, fifos, sockets, and
device nodes. Symlinks are copied as symlinks. Device nodes are recreated.

erasync must run as root, on both hosts of a remote copy: the dm-era
metadata and the era logs are only readable by root. It also needs
era_invalidate from thin-provisioning-tools and user extended attributes
on DEST. The era of each file is stored in the user.erasync.era xattr and
in /var/lib/erasync/era/<device-id>.

```
erasync [-v] [-n] [--delete] [--exclude PATTERN]... [--force[=PATTERN]]...
        SRC [DEST]
```

-v prints each file. -n prints the actions and writes nothing. --delete
removes destination names that are not in the source. --exclude skips
entries that match a shell pattern, and keeps them in the destination
with --delete. It may be given more than once. A pattern without a '/'
matches names at any depth; see erasync(1) for the rest. --force copies
files whole instead of the changed blocks: every file, or with
--force=PATTERN the files that match.

```
erasync -v /vm/images /backup/images
erasync -v /vm/images/disk.qcow2 /backup/images/disk.qcow2
erasync -v /vm/images 10.10.10.11:/backup/images
erasync --exclude='*-tester*.qcow2' /vm/images /backup/images
erasync --force='*-base*.qcow2' /vm/images /backup/images
```

## Config file

Options that should be used on every run can go in
~/.config/erasync/config instead of on the command line. Options before the first `sync` line
apply to every copy; the ones after a `sync SRC DEST` line apply only to
a copy of that SRC to that DEST. One option per line, without shell
quotes:

```
--verbose

sync /vm/images 10.10.10.11:/backup/images
        --exclude *-tester*.qcow2
```

Given only SRC, erasync copies to the DEST of the sync line for that
SRC: `erasync /vm/images` above. erasync prints the DEST and the
options it took from the file, unless -q is given.

`--config FILE` reads another file, and `--no-config` none. See
erasync(1) for the details.

## Design

A copy has a sender, which walks the source tree and decides what each
file needs, and a receiver, which does all the writing at the
destination. For a local copy the sender calls the receiver directly and
the bytes are copied with copy_file_range. For a remote copy the same
calls travel over ssh as frames (see remote.c). Only directory listings
wait for an answer, so a directory costs one round trip however many
files it holds.

The sources:

- sender.c: the tree walk and the plan for each file
- receiver.c: everything that changes the destination
- sync.c: a local copy
- remote.c, proto.c: a remote copy and its frames
- config.c: ~/.config/erasync/config
- era.c: dm-era devices, snapshots and dirty blocks
- record.c: the user.erasync.era xattr, starting with "v1 "
- dm.c: device-mapper tables and messages

The comment at the top of era.c describes when the era records are
written.

## Installing

`make install` installs erasync and its man page under /usr/local.
Set PREFIX, or DESTDIR for packaging, to install elsewhere.

## Testing

`make check` needs root: tests/era.sh builds dm-era devices on loop
files, runs the tree tests on them, and checks incremental copies locally
and over the protocol, in both directions. Without root the dm-era tests
are skipped.

